today on campaign check we're taking a  look at education one of the key  battlegrounds of any election campaign  and this morning the Lib Dem leader Joe  Swinton addressed inequality in the  classroom claiming that the achievement  gap between richer and poorer children  can best be closed in the early years  but under the Conservatives is widening  so is she right that the most  disadvantaged pupils are lagging behind  because of the Tories this chart shows  how many months pupils to get free  school meals are behind their peers and  you can see that by GCSEs poorer pupils  are just over 18 months behind now the  gap has been narrowing which is why this  line is going down and it's ascending in  primary schools too but progress is  slowing and last year the GCSE gap  actually got wider and in early years  which is for children up to five years  old that's what the Lib Dems have been  talking about it's the same story the  gap has been falling a little but lately  it's widened so Joe Swinton is correct  although we should say only just because  the gap that she refers to was  inequality by the end of reception year  it's only widened by the equivalent of  three days and the think-tank that makes  this data says in the early years there  was no substantial change to the gap now  of course this technical discussion  risks missing the point which is that  the gap starts early in life gets larger  and shows no sign of closing so what are  the main pipes going to do about it well  they've all pledged extra money for  schools labour and the Greens more  substantially although the Lib Dems have  also pledged a significant sum to revive  35 hours of free childcare but given the  scale of the gap even the largest sums  are unlikely to make a difference the  impartial Education Policy Institute  suggests that at the current rate of  progress it would take more than 500  years to close the GCC attainment gap  that would take us up to the Year 2581  and when they assess the manifesto the  manifestos they found that the parties  pledges were unlikely to deliver so Joe  Swinson is right to say the attainment  gap is widening  that the earliest is the most effective  place to fix it but if the Lib Dems are  any party just they can close the gap  with their funding of puzzles that claim  will need a lot of scrutiny
 

